@startmindmap GetterSetter_Mindmap
skinparam dpi 150
* When should accessor methods be used?\n(Holub, "Why getter and setter methods are evil", JavaWorld 2003)
** Core problem
*** Getters/setters expose implementation details
**** A private field + public get/set ≈ a public field
**** Change a field's type → every caller breaks
*** They break encapsulation & increase coupling
*** Symptom of procedural thinking in OO code\n(data-centric design, "anemic" objects)
** Holub's principles
*** "Don't ask for the information you need to do the work;\nask the object that has the information to do the work for you"
*** Design from use cases / behaviour (CRC cards),\nnot from data (fields first)
*** Minimise data movement between objects
*** Prefer methods like cart.totalCost(), account.deposit(x)\nover getBalance()/setBalance()
** Related principles (other sources)
*** Tell, Don't Ask (Martin Fowler / Pragmatic Programmers)
*** Law of Demeter: talk only to immediate collaborators
*** Effective Java (Bloch)
**** Item 16: public classes → accessors, not public fields
**** Item 17: minimise mutability → avoid setters
*** Clean Code (R. Martin): objects hide data & expose behaviour;\ndata structures expose data & have no behaviour
left side
** When accessors ARE acceptable
*** Required by the business/use case\n(AIMS: cart must display title, category, cost… → getters)
*** At system boundaries
**** UI / presentation layer
**** Persistence, serialization (JPA, JSON)
**** DTOs / JavaBeans frameworks & tools
*** Getter returns an interface or immutable value,\nnot the internal representation
*** Value objects / records (read-only data)
** When to AVOID them
*** Auto-generating get/set for every field
*** Setters that let callers bypass business rules\n(e.g. setBalance instead of credit/debit)
*** Getters returning mutable internals\n(return a copy or an unmodifiable view)
** Conclusion
*** Accessors are not evil by themselves;\nunjustified accessors are
*** Add an accessor only when a use case needs it;\nprefer behaviour-rich methods
*** Lab02 choice: DVD has getters only;\nsetTitle() is temporary for the pass-by-value test
@endmindmap
